# How CNI assigns an IP to a Pod (Step-by-step).
Real CNI plugin examples:
• Calico
• Flannel
• Cilium

• When a Pod is created, kubelet calls the CNI plugin.
• The CNI plugin picks a free IP address from the cluster’s IP pool.
• It assigns this IP inside the Pod’s network namespace.
• A veth pair is created to connect the Pod to the node network.
• Now the Pod gets a unique IP address.
• Using this IP, the Pod can communicate with other Pods and Services.
What is CRI?

• CRI is a runtime interface that lets Kubernetes talk to container runtimes.container high level container-run time is called to run and run will be create container
• It connects kubelet with the container runtime to run containers.

#How CRI works (simple flow):

• Kubernetes (kubelet) sends a request via CRI.
• The container runtime (like containerd or CRI-O) receives the request.
• The runtime pulls images, creates containers, starts/stops them, and reports status back.
What is CSI?

• CSI is a storage interface that lets Kubernetes talk to storage systems.
• It allows you to attach external storage to Pods.

#How CSI works (simple flow):

• A Pod requests storage using a PersistentVolumeClaim (PVC).
• Kubernetes calls the CSI driver.
• The CSI driver talks to the storage provider and creates/attaches a volume.
• The volume is mounted inside the Pod.
• Now the Pod can read/write persistent data.
#runC is a low-level container runtime that actually starts containers on the host.runC is the low-level runtime that actually runs containers on the OS.
#cgroup:It controls CPU, memory, disk I/O, and network for containers and processes.
• Containers use cgroups to make sure one container doesn’t eat all the resources.
*workflow how to container launch
[pod] kubelet → CRI → runtime → pull image → runC → cgroups → container run

 ##kublet workflow
